WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.610
A query wants merchant 42's total for November 7th.

2
00:00:06.610 --> 00:00:12.589
Two days of payments sit in one Parquet file: 400,000 rows, about 4 megabytes.

3
00:00:12.589 --> 00:00:14.965
How much must the reader touch?

4
00:00:15.167 --> 00:00:19.568
The simplest reader reads all of it, and throws almost everything away.

5
00:00:19.767 --> 00:00:29.309
But Parquet cuts the rows into row groups of 100,000, and inside each group it stores every column on its own, as a column chunk.

6
00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:36.888
Last, the writer adds the footer: where every chunk starts, how big it is, and the smallest and largest value in it.

7
00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:38.995
So a reader starts at the back.

8
00:00:38.995 --> 00:00:44.985
It reads the last 8 bytes to learn the footer's length, then the footer itself, about 2 kilobytes.

9
00:00:45.167 --> 00:00:49.062
This file was written sorted by day, then by merchant.

10
00:00:49.062 --> 00:00:55.697
So the footer says row groups 0 and 1 hold only November 6th, and 2 and 3 only November 7th.

11
00:00:55.900 --> 00:01:02.051
Row groups 0 and 1 can't hold November 7th, so the reader skips them without reading a byte.

12
00:01:02.267 --> 00:01:05.916
Row group 3 holds merchants 250 to 499.

13
00:01:05.916 --> 00:01:10.408
Merchant 42 can't be there, so it's skipped too.

14
00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:15.697
Checking the filter against min and max like this is called predicate pushdown.

15
00:01:15.697 --> 00:01:17.568
Only row group 2 might match.

16
00:01:17.767 --> 00:01:22.431
Inside it, the query needs three columns: merchant, day and amount.

17
00:01:22.431 --> 00:01:25.076
It never touches payment id or status.

18
00:01:25.267 --> 00:01:30.607
So the reader fetches three chunks, as three byte ranges, and filters the rows in them.

19
00:01:30.833 --> 00:01:37.453
That's about 480 kilobytes instead of 4 megabytes, an eighth of the file, for the same answer.

20
00:01:37.667 --> 00:01:40.018
It works because the file is sorted.

21
00:01:40.018 --> 00:01:45.307
Written in random order, every row group would span both days and every merchant.

22
00:01:45.307 --> 00:01:49.356
Nothing could be skipped, and the read grows to 2.4 megabytes.

23
00:01:49.533 --> 00:01:55.706
The footer's min and max let a reader skip row groups, and storing by column lets it skip columns.

24
00:01:55.706 --> 00:01:57.973
Sort the file by what you filter on.
